WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:21.045
And I want to welcome you to a very special topic. And the topic is a movie analysis about The Matrix, the movie from 1999, from back then the Wachowski brothers. And I've been asked about The Matrix and do an analysis on that for a very long time.

00:00:21.431 --> 00:00:43.079
And I was afraid to do it, not because I thought I could not, but rather because The Matrix is a kind of movie that had so much analysis and so many things were said about it that I thought, okay, I, I cannot find an angle which is kind of new. I could not say anything that would be not something that already someone else has said it and likely said it better.

00:00:44.348 --> 00:01:00.171
That changed a little bit because we did a little analysis of a short movie from the Animatrix. Anthology roughly half a year ago, which was really, really cool. It's an 8-minute short movie and it's animated.

00:01:00.783 --> 00:01:22.360
And I think we talked about it 3 or 3 and a half hours in the group, and there were really good ideas from the people that participated. And this was a really good discussion. And I think I found there's some, some angle into the movie that I hope no one else has found. At least in this depth. And this is why I want to now talk about The Matrix.

00:01:22.408 --> 00:01:45.167
So basically people here made it possible. I also watched the movie again just now recently as preparation, and I had Stefan helping me out there a lot as he's also a writer. And yeah, so The Matrix. And I will ignore most analysis and I hope like I have some original things in there.

00:01:45.633 --> 00:02:08.241
The interesting thing is about The Matrix that you can talk about it really a lot. There's a lot of, let's say, philosophical questions posed there. And I think this is the reason why people are still talking about it. It's a question about like, do human beings need to suffer to exist? Can they accept happiness or paradise? What is freedom? What is free will?

00:02:09.573 --> 00:02:30.786
Are human beings like a virus? So it also questions reality and the world that we live in and technology and progress and all these different things. And I think this is why this still captures the imagination of people, even though that the movie is now 25 years old. So it's not even from this century. It's not even from this millennia.

00:02:31.333 --> 00:02:46.054
It's from the past millennia, like the very cusp of Changing of the Millennium. And I chose the subtitle for this as Knowing is Believing for a very specific reason, because of the concept of gnosis.

00:02:47.049 --> 00:03:10.255
And gnosis is very well encapsulated in the quote from the movie, the exchange between Neo and Trinity when they meet for the first time, where Neo asks like, what is The Matrix? And Trinity says, about Morpheus, when I asked him, he said that no one could ever be told the answer to that question. They have to see to believe it.

00:03:12.176 --> 00:03:35.876
And that reminded me of another quote, a very famous quote from Jung. And this is from a BBC interview where he was asked whether he believes in God. And the answer he gave was, I don't need to believe. I know. And we did a discussion about this interview already some time ago, and it was also a very, very good discussion.

00:03:36.054 --> 00:03:52.125
And this opens up a lot of questions like, okay, how can you not believe in God, but rather say like, you know, and the concept that's behind that is the concept of gnosis, which means knowledge is from ancient Greek.

00:03:52.939 --> 00:04:16.088
And it refers to direct experiential knowledge and that it does more, like it's more about a transformation. It's about what it does to the person to know something similar as, for example, knowing that there's a God or knowing that there's The Matrix. And there's a whole, let's say, philosophy behind it. And the philosophy is called Gnosticism.

00:04:17.031 --> 00:04:43.898
And I have a quote here from Jung for that, which goes: The Gnostics held that this god was a vain inferior demon who in his vanity had created man imperfect and unconscious, and that therefore it was a mighty good thing when the foreign god of the spiritual worlds interfered with the doings of that creator and sent his son into paradise in the form of the serpent to redeem man, to teach him consciousness.

00:04:44.830 --> 00:05:08.513
So the redemption was simply the continuation of the separation. The distinction, the separation was then understood as a good thing inasmuch as it was a separation from something that was evil. They understood it from the side of individuation. So there's already a lot of stuff in here. So the Gnostics are really old, they're almost 2,000 years old.

00:05:10.022 --> 00:05:27.877
They posed a specific question. They asked, okay, if God is perfect, then why did he create the world so imperfectly? Why is there suffering? Why is like bad things happening? So they had the idea that not God the creator created the world, but rather like a lesser being, the Demiurge.

00:05:28.775 --> 00:05:48.810
And the Demiurge created an incomplete world, a corrupted world in a sense. So it is kind of vital to wake up from that world, to realize it, and to free oneself in a way from it. So the stories in the Bible become a completely different meaning.

00:05:48.938 --> 00:06:07.261
Like in the traditional sense, when you have Genesis and Adam and Eve get expelled from paradise, this is seen like, oh, this is the mortal sin of humans and they sinned against God and now they have to toil in the world. And labor under sweat and blood for their daily bread and all these different things.

00:06:07.646 --> 00:06:30.485
And that the snake is associated with Satan that tempted Eve and Adam into being expelled from paradise. And the Gnostics say, no, no, no, well, the world is wrong. So knowledge and consciousness is a good thing. And actually the snake is the savior, which is a very interesting thought. And you could believe this is heresy.

00:06:32.208 --> 00:06:53.036
We don't have so much left from the Gnostics. Most of it got destroyed, but we have some fragments left and we have elaborations from the church about the Gnostics. And the thing is, like, you cannot really keep ideas down. Things are popping up. And this is what this event will be about.

00:06:53.758 --> 00:07:09.147
It's about, there's certain ideas that come up again and again and again, and they get kind of re-explored. New and new and new, because this is what human beings do and how they experience the world. So we will go through these points here.

00:07:09.212 --> 00:07:31.018
We will go through the thing there, the creature that brings consciousness, and we will go through individuation and how much it actually fits for The Matrix. So the question is, what makes a story Gnostic? Like, how did that come to this point to say like, oh yeah, this is a Gnostic story. And there's some points for that.

00:07:31.898 --> 00:07:49.408
So for example, that the world is a prison or an illusion, similar to the cave in Plato's allegory, where people are watching the shadows on the wall thinking that's a real thing, but the philosopher breaks out, sees the sun, sees the truth and says like, oh, that's a false light there in the cave and tries to free people from that.

00:07:50.950 --> 00:08:11.064
There's a hidden ruler or false god in this world that keeps up this illusion. And the hero, so the main figure for the story, has an existential discomfort about how things are. They're feeling that there's something wrong and they feel out of place and they say, okay, there's something not right, something that needs to be rectified.

00:08:12.364 --> 00:08:25.507
And the salvation happens through knowledge and an internal awakening to become aware of the world and how it really is. And get transformed in the process of that.

00:08:26.324 --> 00:08:42.787
And what we have here on the right is a really old and famous picture from the Middle Ages showing like how people break out of the world, this world that they see and which is kind of a world of illusion to see beyond this, to see the, the greater thing, the, let's say the real world.

00:08:43.796 --> 00:09:04.200
And this as a story concept might already pop up some ideas in your mind that reminds you of certain stories. And it was a very, very, very popular topic around the shifts of the millennia. And you had such movies as The Truman Show or Pleasantville or Dark City or 13th Floor.

00:09:04.910 --> 00:09:28.718
And also really a lot of stories from Philip K. Dick revolve around these ideas, these Gnostic ideas that fit this description as it described here, up to the point that when Matrix came out, that it got sued a lot. Like a lot of people said, hey, wait, this, this is my idea. People are living in a situation and they're trying to free themselves.

00:09:29.810 --> 00:09:51.324
There were some lawsuits that are still going on actually about how much the Wachowski brothers stole. And then it's a question, okay, how much is actually like stealing, taking things, and how much is coming from, let's say, the same source. Because Jung says, we in our psyche, we have a collective unconscious.

00:09:51.726 --> 00:10:09.941
That's a layer of psychology, which is common to all human beings as they exist now, as they existed in the past and how they will exist in the future. And when we do creative work, we draw from that source. It's not only that we draw from this source, but this is also The things that people like the most.

00:10:10.038 --> 00:10:18.277
So when something is very popular and it speaks to a lot of people, it's archetypal. This is why we have love songs or horror stories and all these different things.

00:10:19.016 --> 00:10:42.177
Jung sees there an entanglement between our psychology and the cultural artifacts or creations that we have in our culture, in our life, and that we like to experience because it's in a way experiencing our own psyche. It speaks to us. It's reaching into us and it's activating something.

00:10:45.544 --> 00:11:07.945
So it is that The Matrix on the one side is an amalgamation about a lot of things. As the screenwriters wrote about it 10 years, they wrote on the script for 10 years and nobody wanted to do it because When you read it, it's like already pretty expensive with all the special effects and so on, but it was also a very wild idea and they were unproven.

00:11:07.993 --> 00:11:30.015
They only had one movie before, it was called Bound. And, but this gave them time to like work through it again and again and again and again and incorporate really a lot of influences that they had and that they experienced around this time, this Zeitgeist. So this collective spirit of the time. That captured people in their imagination.

00:11:31.419 --> 00:11:53.537
This also explains why the first Matrix movie was so good with amazing lore, amazing ideas and worldbuilding, all these different things. And the second Matrix movie was not quite up to the task because it did not have so much time. That is how it is in Hollywood. If you want to make money, you have to make it quick. So let's talk about the serpent.

00:11:53.619 --> 00:12:10.173
Let's talk about the figure that appears and brings consciousness to the unconscious creatures in this false prison world. And in the story, that's Morpheus. And of course, I have some quotes about that from Jung.

00:12:11.880 --> 00:12:31.127
The inner voice, as I have explained above, makes us conscious of the evil from which the whole community is suffering, whether it be the nation or the whole human race. The voice that Jung is talking about is the psychological concept of the Self. Jung says we are not alone in our psyche.

00:12:31.608 --> 00:12:53.689
We have a very complex psychological apparatus as human beings that is kind of in different layers so that we have our consciousness, we have our personal unconscious, and we have our collective unconscious. But those are not dead layers. Those are not just like places where you go, but those are living, breathing things that can also interact with you.

00:12:54.315 --> 00:13:18.083
And he says one central concept within this psychological structure is the self. And the self is a kind of combination of everything which is in the psyche. You can think about it as a kind of future you. So when you have, for example, have an acorn, the tree is already in the acorn. You cannot see it. Right? But it unfolds over time.

00:13:20.456 --> 00:13:40.403
And the Jungian view is on it that the tree calls out to the acorn to let it grow in a certain way. So the psychological development of, of us as human beings, as individuals, as persons, is that there is a psychological force within us.

00:13:41.006 --> 00:13:55.925
That is kind of steering us in a certain direction and it's guiding us in a certain way. He says there's a lot of voices and there are a lot of things trying to steer us, but there's, for example, emotions or archetypes and all these different things.

00:13:55.989 --> 00:14:18.297
But he says like, yeah, like one of those voices is actually on our side and that this is also the basis for a lot of religious experiences and religions that there is this voice calling to us. Socrates had something similar with his daemon. And in the movie, this is exemplified by Morpheus.

00:14:19.350 --> 00:14:42.175
He represents the Self for Neo as the main character, and he speaks to Neo. He's that voice. And how does that voice come? Through text on a screen, through a telephone. So completely disembodied. Voice that is there, then this is not only represented with Morpheus for the first time.

00:14:42.239 --> 00:15:06.168
This has been represented very often in human history. And for the alchemists, for example, this was through Mercurius, the Greek messenger of the gods. And this is why Sean Connery is here. Also represented very often as an old man, for example, Gandalf in Lord of the Rings. So this is what Jung talks about here.

00:15:07.056 --> 00:15:22.909
Archetype of the old man. Whenever he appears, he also refers to a certain situation. There's some disorientation, a certain unconsciousness. People are in a sort of confusion and don't know what to do. It is this voice.

00:15:24.390 --> 00:15:45.863
That's represented by Morpheus, that we can say like, when we have a cultural creation as a theater play, book, or even a drawing, like anything, that Jung says this is our psyche expressing itself, which means we have there externalized a lot of things that are happening inside of us, like a theater, like a play.

00:15:47.003 --> 00:16:09.557
So to have then the interaction between Morpheus and Neo is the interaction between the Self and, let's say, the individual consciousness of ego. And what Morpheus does in this story is to bring people into consciousness, similar as the snake in the Gnostic text, bringing people out of paradise, out of their unconsciousness.

00:16:10.088 --> 00:16:28.968
And interestingly enough, what does Morpheus wear in the movie? He wears a leather coat. In a snakeskin, which is quite interesting. Of course, he does not only do it to Neo. He also does it to a bunch of other characters.

00:16:33.055 --> 00:16:57.303
The characters being Cypher and in an indirect way also Agent Smith. The problem with consciousness is The thing that, let's say, makes us human creates also a lot of suffering. Like being aware of things and paying attention to things means that you also not pay attention to other things.

00:16:58.648 --> 00:17:20.997
And a big part of growing up is becoming, let's say, aware of the suffering of the world and how incomplete the world is, or even worse, how incomplete oneself is. And you have Cypher that got the call from the Self, from Morpheus, about the truth and the real world and got the choice to learn about it. And he learned about it and he wants to go back.

00:17:21.061 --> 00:17:44.794
And this is how he's willing to betray not only his immediate friends, but let's say the possibility of human beings to get out of the Matrix because Morpheus is the way how to do that. And this is what this quote is fitting very, very well for. We believe that mentally ill persons aren't happy. Not at all.

00:17:45.485 --> 00:18:08.132
They don't want to get out at all. I once treated someone. She was in it too. I got her out using all the tricks of the trade. She explained to me, now you've got me out of here into this filthy world. I don't want to be here. Now I can't go back. You see, I had locked the door behind her, but she would much rather have been insane. And this is a situation of Cypher.

00:18:08.454 --> 00:18:27.688
He's negotiating with the agents to go back because he wants the illusions. He wants the fakeness. He does not want to be confronted with the real world and all the things that are associated with that. He rather wants to be asleep. He does not want to be awake. And absolutely interestingly enough, that's also the motivation for Agent Smith.

00:18:28.449 --> 00:18:49.960
This is what he tells Morpheus, so the Self, during the interrogation scene. He says he cannot stand this human world. He cannot stand everything about it. Like, he's a computer program, but he has to go in this kind of mortal world and be bound by the rules of it and experience it, and says he's sick and tired of it.

00:18:50.332 --> 00:19:13.423
He wants his job done, and this is why he wants the codes to Zion, so he can end the whole charade. The agents are there to hunt down the human beings that are awake. So they have a task, they have a goal, and they have to bring them down. Until they bring them down, they have to be kind of conscious. They have to be also kind of outside the program itself.

00:19:13.824 --> 00:19:37.218
You see that with Agent Smith having his own thoughts, his own ideas. And going also his own way. In a sense, Morpheus also made him conscious about the Matrix, but he doesn't want that. And this reminded me of something that I've seen in Rick and Morty, which is this figure of Mr. Meeseeks, which is also a very good analogy to consciousness and how consciousness works.

00:19:37.764 --> 00:19:56.058
Like Jung said, the best way to become conscious of a thing is to hit your head. Consciousness is there to mitigate a problem. So you live your life, you do it normally, you drive a car, you're in a relationship, you use your computer, and suddenly something acts up, something does not work.

00:19:56.699 --> 00:20:15.416
So you become jolted awake, and now you are kind of confused and you have to figure stuff out. And this is like very, very tiring. This can be very, very tiring and scary. And a lot of things, can happen. Like, ah, I would rather not have this problem. But the question is what to do. The thing is the problem needs to be confronted.

00:20:16.556 --> 00:20:40.233
And similarly, in Rick and Morty, the figure of Mr. Meeseeks, they have to do things and solve things. Like for example, Meeseeks gets the task, bring me a beer. So it comes into existence, brings the beer, and then disappears. And in the episode, they have to teach Jerry, a character in the show How to Golf, and he's just so bad at it that he does not learn how to golf.

00:20:41.370 --> 00:20:57.594
So Mr. Meeseeks just stays awake, awake, awake, awake, and he says like, this is existence is pain. Like, I just want to go back. And this is what is then with Agent Smith, who's kind of representing an evil spirit that pops up in the psyche.

00:20:57.642 --> 00:21:22.045
Jung talks about autonomous psychological forces in the psyche of people that comes up and brings people to do something. And you see that in the movie, like people turn into Agent Smith, they become kind of activated. And Jung sees this also with complexes or archetypes or emotions that people suddenly become very impersonal.

00:21:23.363 --> 00:21:45.906
Emotions are not personal. Emotions are not you. Like anger, lust, sadness. That's not the human person. It's in the sphere of human experience, but it can completely take people over. Like an idea, like an ideology, like fascism, communism, or many fanatic strains of religion. It's not about the individual person.

00:21:46.002 --> 00:22:10.912
It's about more of a system that's trying to keep itself alive. Similar as Agent Smith. But Agent Smith, he wants to break out of the programming and he wants his existence ceased. Which brings us to the last character, which is important in the story, which is Trinity, like the right hand of Morpheus and the love interest for Neo.

00:22:13.081 --> 00:22:26.892
And interestingly enough, I have a quote about her role in the movie. Also from Jung again: The old wise man represents the wisdom of the serpent, as the anima represents its movement and cunning.

00:22:27.517 --> 00:22:44.297
There is really very little difference between the old wise man and the anima, and therefore the two are so often together, as in the myth of Simon Magus and Helena and the idea of the Sibyl or somnambule and the wise man. This was pronounced horribly.

00:22:46.433 --> 00:23:09.482
But Jung says when you have your psyche, you go through kind of figures that you encounter representing the different parts of your psyche. The first one is the shadow, uh, which is the personal unconscious, which is kind of your personal problems that you have. And afterwards, you're confronted more with the collective. You're more confronted with fundamental things.

00:23:09.999 --> 00:23:30.344
And like a fundamental thing in the psychology of a man as for example Neo, is his femininity, is his female side. And this represented in man is through the anima, a figure that represents their female side, but also more as it's connecting to the collective part of the psyche.

00:23:31.852 --> 00:23:55.221
So it's an interesting, when you see it in the movie, it's an interesting that this is how stories get written and how people like to experience them. Because who does Neo encounter first? It's the anima. Similar as in Jungian psychology. Jung says, first you encounter shadow, then the anima, and then the Self. And the anima being basically the female side of the Self.

00:23:57.540 --> 00:24:13.035
So it is Trinity that creates the contact and brings the Neo to Morpheus. Which is the Self. Which brings us to Neo and his journey. And his journey is that of individuation.

00:24:13.147 --> 00:24:36.622
Individuation means becoming an individual, meaning being separated from things, which doesn't mean like completely separated from human relationships or the world or anything, or from emotions, but rather integrated. Aware and awake enough to realize that you are not the role that you play in society.

00:24:36.767 --> 00:24:50.445
You are not the emotions that you have inside of you. You are not the thoughts that you have inside of you. That you're not identical with them and just act them out, let's say, but rather reflect on them and realize what is personal and what is not personal.

00:24:51.770 --> 00:25:16.298
And this process is symbolically represented in the movie through like constant waking up If you pay attention to the, in the movie, there's a lot of scenes where Neo is just waking up. He's waking up when he looked at the monitor. He wakes up after they instill the, uh, like the robot in his belly button. And also he is waking up from the Matrix, right? In his pod.

00:25:17.634 --> 00:25:38.564
And it is that the Self is showing Neo the way, but he always has to do the decision. He always have to. Make a choice which way to go. So this is what a lot of scenes in the beginning are about. Like he's asked, oh, you want to come to the party? And he follows the white rabbit to the party. His boss asks him whether he wants to keep his job or not.

00:25:39.784 --> 00:26:02.427
And also then the interesting story, like when it's the first time contact with Morpheus and it's this disembodied voice coming, telling him what to do, trying to escape, having the superior knowledge about the situation. And the agents approaching him and say like, this is how you escape and this is what you have to do. Neo is not able to do it because he does not yet believe.

00:26:02.523 --> 00:26:26.686
He does not yet know. He's not in this psychological frame of mind to follow those orders. And Jung says what the Self will pose to us, to the ego, will seem strange and foreign. Same as for Socrates, his Self, his daimon, For example, told him you need to play the flute and he could not play the flute, so he just started making a horrible thing out of it.

00:26:27.553 --> 00:26:41.990
But it also told him to stay and not flee Athens, even though that he would be killed for that. And this is one of the big reasons why we know Socrates today, because he did not run. He listened to his inner voice.

00:26:44.370 --> 00:27:07.458
So the individuation process has to do with separation from things and like one way to break free from the collective, the collectiveness, is through a secret society. And human beings really, really, really like secret societies with secret knowledge and mysteries and all these different things. So the secret society is an intermediary stage on the way to individuation.

00:27:07.891 --> 00:27:30.329
The individual is still relying on a collective organization to affect his differentiation for him. That is, he has not yet recognized that it is really the individual's task to differentiate himself from all the others and stand on his own feet. And this is what's happening to Neo. Neo is being introduced into a new society, into a new world. And this is the resistance.

00:27:30.538 --> 00:27:42.880
This is Morpheus and his team of the Nebuchadnezzar. So he's learning there new things, the way how to do and how to behave. For example, don't fight the agents. Run away.

00:27:45.367 --> 00:28:02.350
And Neo follows that, but even though that Morpheus is showing him the way and he has to make the choices and being active about it, individuation is always about action, it's still not enough. He has to kind of transcend also that.

00:28:03.808 --> 00:28:22.204
And this is then when he kind of goes against the secret society when he wants to save Morpheus and say like, no, I want to save him. I want to confront the agents. And everybody's saying, ah, this is madness. This is absolutely crazy. And he says, ah, no, I know it will work out. Just, I have to do it.

00:28:23.975 --> 00:28:40.930
And this is then the interesting point in the story because Morpheus as a figure, as a guiding light, kind of disappears. Now Neo has to stand on his own feet. And this relationship is very similar to what Jung described between a therapist and a patient.

00:28:41.753 --> 00:28:59.964
So when a patient would come to Jung describing the problems of their lives, Jung said he would take a kind of helpful position of this inner voice that people have inside of them with a goal that this contact, this connection to the inner voice is reestablished.

00:29:00.576 --> 00:29:22.188
That people don't want to stay in Wonderland and kind of ignore the things that are going on, but rather pay attention to that and follow that. And this is what he describes here. All of us have our best analyst within ourselves, and therefore I try to bring forth his inner voice in everyone that I treat, which then replaces me. I can leave.

00:29:23.135 --> 00:29:42.950
The voice does everything in my place. So there is a kind of structure and harmony within the psyche, which can be broken, but also reestablished. And this is then what's symbolized also in the story of The Matrix. So Neo does this.

00:29:43.159 --> 00:30:04.862
Neo follows the voice of the Self, acts on his own feet, makes his own decisions. And shows that an alternative way of living is possible. Like even to the people who are awake from the Matrix and know like everything, even they are made aware like, okay, you can confront the agents. You can put up a fight.

00:30:06.244 --> 00:30:30.147
So it's interesting that their consciousness also gets expanded by Neo. Maybe that's why he is the one. But first, he's not the one. This is what he's being told. And it's right. Like the way how Neo is, He is not the one. Like, he has a good heart, as the oracle says, but he has to do more, much more. And interestingly enough, again, this is a Gnostic idea.

00:30:31.766 --> 00:30:42.858
And this is what this quote is about. The Gnostic idea of Christ was that he was not born a Christ. He had to be a twice-born, baptized in the Jordan, in order to become the real God-Christ.

00:30:43.683 --> 00:31:07.400
According to this tradition, The Holy Ghost generated the body of the man Christ by the miraculous impregnation of the Virgin, but he was still only an ordinary man, though his generation was a miracle. So Neo has to become the one, and he has to die to be reborn then into the one, the savior of the human race, the one that Morpheus has been looking for.

00:31:08.843 --> 00:31:25.060
So he becomes transformed and transfigured. And there's another tradition in the West that deals with that, which is that of alchemy, that Jung also very closely investigated because of its psychological value. This is what this quote is about.

00:31:26.007 --> 00:31:48.323
In the Middle Ages, philosophy—this means alchemy—prevailed over the fact that such an extent that the base metal lead was credited with the power to turn into gold under certain conditions. And the dark psychic man with the capacity to turn himself into a higher pneumatic man. But just as lead, which theoretically could become gold.

00:31:50.640 --> 00:32:14.394
The idea in alchemy is really that the gold is already in the lead. It's a chemical process that brings forth something. There's a kind of spirit trapped in there. There's a spirit trapped in matter, and the material world that needs to be set free by human beings. And the alchemist does this through himself, through changing himself.

00:32:15.052 --> 00:32:39.354
He transforms the lead into gold, which is a symbolic representation showing like psychological transformation in Jung's eyes. But this is a similar story with Neo, right? He's a normal everyday man at the beginning. Has a job, is a hacker at night, and then he becomes the savior of the human race, but only because he goes the way of individuation and he is then reborn.

00:32:40.790 --> 00:33:05.255
And a very, very interesting idea there is that Trinity kisses him at the right point, like his anima, also by Jung described as a soul image, reawakens him. And believes in him. And when I saw this, I was reminded by a quote from Robert A. Johnson in a book that he wrote.

00:33:05.303 --> 00:33:26.663
